# Two-Dimension (2D) Cu-MOFs/aptamer Nanoprobe for In Situ ATP Imaging in Living Cells

Xinxin Shi1 $\cdot^{\cdot}$ Hui ${\sf x u}^{1}$ · Yanan Wu1 $\cdot^{\cdot}$ Yanmin Zhao1 $\cdot\cdot$ Hong‑Min Meng1 $\cdot$ Zhaohui Li1 $\cdot$ Lingbo Qu1
Abstract
Monitoring the intracellular adenosine triphosphate (ATP) level is vital for elaborating its function in physiological states. However, the intracellular fluorescence sensing of ATP using ATP aptamer remains difficult owing to non-target displacement and susceptibility toward enzymatic degradation. Herein, by combining 2D $\mathrm{Cu}$ -MOF nanosheets and FAM labelled ATP aptamer, we developed a fluorescent Cu-MOFs/aptamer nanoprobe to image and sense intracellular ATP. This nanoprobe reveals a very low fluorescence intensity because of the excellent quenching efficiency of 2D $\mathtt{C u}$ -MOF nanosheets. The presence of ATP was capable to dissociate the FAM-aptamer from Cu-MOF nanosheets and resulted in an intense fluorescence signal. The Cu-MOFs/aptamer nanoprobe enables highly sensitive and selective measurement of ATP level ranging from $10\upmu\mathrm{M}$ to $800\upmu\mathrm{M}$ with a detection limit of $4.24\upmu\mathrm{M}$ . This nanosystem also further realized in situ detection of the undulation of ATP trigged by drug stimulation, depending on the selective delivery of the nanoprobe and attractive capability of resisting nonspecific displacement. The constructed nanoprobe may supply a potential platform in clinical diagnostics and biological studies.
Keywords  Metal–organic frameworks $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{\varepsilon}$ Aptamer $\cdot$ ATP $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{\varepsilon}$ Bio-sensing $\cdot\cdot$ Cell imaging
## 1  Introduction
ATP is a multifunctional biomolecule in almost all animate beings [1, 2]. As “molecular unit of currency” for cellular energy transduction, ATP plays a vital role as energy source for cellular metabolism and biochemical processes, participating in regulating cell movement, enzyme catalysis, and ion channels [2–4]. Importantly, intracellular ATP has also been recognized as a significant chemical signaling agent of cellular viability and injuries. The abnormal level of ATP induces various diseases, for example, ischemia, hypoxia, Parkinson’s syndrome and malignant tumors [5–7].
Therefore, exploiting nanoprobe to monitor the ATP level in cells is urgently necessary to elaborate its function in physiological states. Because aptamers have attractive merits, such as specifically binding to targets and easiness of synthesizing and modifying with fluorophores, various aptamer-based fluorescence ATP probes were constructed to advance the sensitivity and selectivity in ATP bioanalysis [8–10]. Considering poor transportation into cells of aptamer probes, several nanomaterials such as gold nanoparticles (AuNPs) [11], $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets [12] and graphene oxide nanosheets [13, 14] were employed as nanocarriers of aptamer probe for cellular ATP sensing. Despite encouraging successes, these nanocarriers still have some defects. For example, the interaction between aptamer and AuNPs is based on the Au-SH assembly or covalent cross-link, which cannot disconnect from nanomaterials after entering into cells [11]. Hence, it is essential to explore new nanocarriers to further apply aptamer-based fluorescence ATP assay in living cells.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of crystalline polymers building from self-assembly of bridging metal ions or clusters with organic ligands [15–21]. Their natural features such as optional pore size, large surface area and adjustable structure and functions have attracted researchers in many fields, such as drug delivery [22], therapy [21, 23], bioimaging [24], and biosensing [25], etc. Recently, various fluorescence nanoprobes physically absorbed on MOFs have been established for ATP sensing [26–30]. For example, ZIF-8 and ZIF-90 have been reported as nanocarriers for fluorescence probe such as Rhodamine B (Rh B) and as analytical platform for fluorescence detection of ATP in living cells [28–30]. However, ZIF-8 owns unique pH-sensitive degradation property, resulting in the leakage of the fluorescence probe and false-positive imaging signals in the acidic environment of cancer cells [31].To improve the ability of specific targeting ATP for cellular analysis, Yang’s group reported a stable MIL/aptamer nanosensor in the acidic cellular environment to image intracellular ATP [27]. Despite their merit of resisting nonspecific displacement by nonspecific biomolecules, notably nonspecific proteins in living cells, traditional three-dimensional (3D) MOF/aptamer still have some drawbacks in biosensors, including poor dispersion and low available active sites [32]. Compared with bulk 3D MOFs, 2D MOF nanosheets possess a large surface area that exposes more accessible active sites. MOF nanosheets consequently display strong adsorption affinity, excellent fluorescence-quenching ability and low background signal with enhanced sensitivity [15, 32]. However, there are few explorations of Cu-MOF nanosheets for in situ imaging intracellular ATP molecules.
Herein, a stable Cu-MOFs/aptamer fluorescent ATP probe that enables resist nonspecific displacement was developed for monitoring and imaging intracellular ATP level (Fig. 1). The Cu-MOFs/aptamer was constructed by loading the FAM labelled ATP aptamer onto $\mathtt{C u}$ -MOF nanosheets, leading to the fluorescence quenching of FAM through fluorescence resonance energy transfer (FRET) effect. With an increase of ATP, the fluorescence intensity of FAM was markedly recovered according to the conformational and structural changes resulted from the interaction between the aptamer and ATP molecules. This developed $\mathtt{C u}$ -MOFs/aptamer nanoprobe exhibits high sensitivity and selectivity to ATP over the range from $10\upmu\mathrm{M}$ to $800~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ . The detection limit was calculated to be $4.24~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ . Furthermore, the capability of resisting nonspecific displacement of this nanosystem reduced the false-positive signal and realized further in situ monitoring and imaging of ATP in living cells.
## 2  Experimental Section
## 2.1  Materials
$\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) were purchased from Macklin Biochemical Co., Ltd. (Shanghai,
China). Meso-Tetra(4-carboxyphenyl) porphine (TCPP), $\mathrm{CF}_{3}\mathrm{COOH}$ $(99\%)$ and etoposide were bought from Energy Chemical Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). Ethanol, N, N-dimethylformamide and (DMF) were obtained from Kermel Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. (Tianjin, China). ATP aptamer ${\boldsymbol{5}}^{\prime}$ -FAM-AAC​CTG​GGG​GAG​TAT​TGC​GGA​GGA AG GT- $3^{\prime}$ , DMEM and thiazolyl blue tetrazolium bromide (MTT) were bought from Sangon Biotech Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). PBS was purchased from Sigma-Aldrich Chemical Co., Ltd. (St. Louis, MO.USA). Oligomycin, from Streptomyces diastatochro-mogenes, was obtained from $\circleddash$ Copyright J&K Scientific Ltd.
## 2.2  Characterizations
ZEN-3700 size analyzer (Enigma Business Park, U.K.) was used to analyze the zeta potential and diameter of nanoparticles. SEM image was captured with Sigma scanning electron microscope (Carl Zeiss, UK). FTIR spectrum was measured with FT-IR Spectrometer (Nicolet iS50, USA). XRD spectrum was obtained with X-ray diffractometer (PANalytical, Almelo, Netherlands). Fluorescence results were supplied with F-7100 spectrophotometer (Hitachi, Tokyo, Japan). Cell viability was operated on TECAN-SPARK microplate reader (Tecan Austria GmbH, A-5082, Grŏdig, Austria). Ultra-pure water was supplied by Milli- $\cdot{\bf Q}^{\circledast}$ IQ 7015 Purification System (Molsheim, France) with an electric resistance of 18.2 MΩ·cm. Cell images of HeLa cells were captured by confocal Laser scanning microscope (CLSM) (Leica $\operatorname{SP8}\mathrm{X}$ ， Wetzlar, Germany).
## 2.3  Synthesis of Cu‑MOF Nanosheets
The Cu-MOFs were synthesized through a solvothermal method [15]. Briefly, $36\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{Cu(NO_{3})_{2}}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $100\mathrm{mg}$ PVP was dissolved in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF/ethanol (the volume ratio was 3:1), respectively. Then $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $\mathrm{CF}_{3}\mathrm{COOH}$ (1 M) was mixed with the above solution. Next, $40\mathrm{mg}$ TCPP was dissolved in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF/ethanol and dropped into the above mixture with stirring. After 15 min of ultrasound, the mixture was put into a round-bottomed flask and heated at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ . To purify the product, the precipitate was centrifuged at $9000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $15\mathrm{min}$ , washed with ethanol for 3 times and dried in vacuum for $10\mathrm{{h}}$ at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Finally, the obtained material was dispersed into water for subsequent use.
## 2.4  Preparation of Cu‑MOFs/aptamer Sensing Platform
The Cu-MOFs/aptamer nanocomplex developed in this work was prepared by mixing $150~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ Cu-MOFs and
$100{\mathrm{nM}}$ FAM-aptamer into PBS buffer $\mathrm{i0}\mathrm{mM})$ ) and shaking at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ . The formed conjugate was harvested by centrifugation at $13,000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $15\mathrm{min}$ and reserved at $4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for the subsequent assays. The fluorescence quenching capacity of $\mathrm{Cu}$ -MOFs was examined by mixing $100{\mathrm{nM}}$ FAM-aptamer with ${\mathrm{Cu-MOF}}$ nanosheets in the range of 0 to $250~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ in PBS buffer ( $10~\mathrm{mM}$ , $\mathrm{pH}6.5$ ) to measure the fluorescence intensity of $520\mathrm{nm}$ with excitation at $488~\mathrm{nm}$ . The loading efficiency of the FAM-aptamer on $\mathbf{Cu-MOF}$ nanosheets was acquired by examining the quantity of the remaining FAM-aptamers in the supernatant solution at $520\mathrm{nm}$ .
## 2.5  Fluorescence Detection of ATP
To detect ATP, Cu-MOFs/aptamer nanoprobe ( $\mathbf{100_{\nM}}$ FAM-aptamer equiv.) was mixed with different concentration of ATP (10, 25, 50, 75, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, and $800~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{h}$ . After centrifugation at $13,000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $15~\mathrm{min}$ , fluorescence spectra of supernatant were collected for quantitatively examination using an FL-7100 from 509 to $650\mathrm{nm}$ with excitation at $488\mathrm{nm}$ . To investigate the selectivity, different nucleotide (CTP, GTP, UTP) $(800~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ were prepared in ultrapure water. Fluorescence spectra were recorded after different interfering substances were added to Cu-MOFs/aptamer nanocomplex ( $100{\mathrm{nM}}$ FAM-aptamer equiv.) at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{h}$ .
## 2.6  Cell Culture
HeLa cells (Human cervical adenocarcinoma epithelial) were maintained with high glucose DMEM that
supplemented with $10\%$ fetal bovine serum (FBS), $100\mathrm{U}/$ mL penicillin and $100\mathrm{mg/mL}$ streptomycin at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a $5\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ humidified environment.
## 2.7  Cell Viability Assays
To assess the viability of the HeLa cells, MTT experiment was performed [33]. Briefly, HeLa cells were first seeded into 96-well plate at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . Then, HeLa cells were treated with various concentrations of Cu-MOFs and Cu-MOFs/aptamer for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ and then changed into the fresh DMEM that contained $0.5~\mathrm{mg}/\$ mL MTT at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ . Afterwards, DMSO was used to dissolve the purple precipitation in the wells and the absorbance at $570~\mathrm{nm}$ was determined by a microplate reader.
## 2.8  Confocal Microscopy Imaging
The cell imaging study of HeLa cells were performed by immersing them in glass-bottom cell culture dishes with DMEM media at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , the cells were cultured with $100~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ etoposide [3, 12]. After $30~\mathrm{min}$ , the cells were washed twice with D-PBS and treated with Cu-MOFs/Aptamer ( $\mathbf{\Gamma}_{100\ n M}$ FAM-aptamer equiv.) for $6\mathrm{n}$ . Then the cells were imaged by confocal microscopy. For oligomycin experiments, the cells cultured with $3~{\upmu\up g}/$ mL oligomycin for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ were incubated with Cu-MOFs/ Aptamer ( $\mathrm{i00~nM}$ FAM-aptamer equiv.) for $6\mathrm{~h~}$ [3]. The treated cells were washed with D-PBS and imaged by confocal microscopy.
## 3  Results and Discussion
## 3.1  Characterization
The morphology and structure of $\mathtt{C u}$ -MOFs are studied by SEM, XRD, DLS and FTIR. It can be observed in SEM image and XRD pattern (Fig. 2a, b) that the well-dispersed crystallinity of Cu-MOFs exhibit nanosheet morphology.
The average hydrodynamic diameter of the Cu-MOFs is measured to be $193.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ by DLS analysis (Fig. 2c). What’s more, by comparing the FTIR spectra of TCPP and Cu-MOFs (Fig. 2d), it can be observed clearly that the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{N-H}}$ stretching vibration peak of TCPP at $965~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ changes to the $\mathrm{{Cu-N}}$ stretching of Cu-MOFs at $999\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , indicating the coordination between $\mathtt{C u}$ and porphyrin centres of TCPP [15].
Due to the absorption ability of Cu-MOFs to singlestranded DNA sequences [32], FAM-Aptamer was labelled to the $\mathrm{Cu}$ -MOFs nanosheets by simple self-assembly, which could be confirmed by the reduced zeta potential of $\mathtt{C u}$ - MOFs/aptamer (Fig. 2e). In addition, the result that the hydrodynamic diameter of Cu-MOFs/aptamer increased to $370.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ was also consistent with the generation of a noncovalent conjugate of Cu-MOFs/aptamer because of the binding between the unpaired nucleobases and the $\pi$ -electron system of TCPP ligand (Fig. 2c) [32]. Nevertheless, the well-overlapped fluorescence spectrum of FAM-Aptamer and the absorption spectrum of $\mathrm{cu}$ -MOFs in Fig. 2f indicates the feasibility of energy transfer from FAM-Aptamer to Cu-MOFs.
Then, the sensing ability of Cu-MOFs/aptamer to ATP was investigated by fluorescence assay. As shown in Fig. 3a, the strong fluorescence of FAM-Aptamer (curve black) is markedly quenched after the addition of $\mathrm{Cu}.$ MOFs (curve red), revealing the excellent binding between Cu-MOFs and ssDNA. After adding ATP, the green fluorescence of FAM is recovered (curve blue), which could be ascribed to the formation of the rigid duplex complex weakening the $\pi$ -interaction between Cu-MOFs nanocarrier and FAM-Aptamer. These results suggest the feasibility of the ATP sensing ability of this Cu-MOFs/aptamer nanoprobe.
## 3.2  Sensing Performance of Cu‑MOFs/aptamer to ATP
To optimize the performance of the Cu-MOFs/aptamer to ATP, different concentrations of $\mathrm{Cu}$ -MOFs with $100~\mathrm{nM}$ FAM-aptamer were prepared. Figure  3b shows that the fluorescence intensity of FAM-Aptamer is reduced as the increased concentration of ${\mathrm{Cu-MOFs}}$ , confirming the absorption of FAM-aptamer on the surface of ${\mathrm{Cu-MOFs}}$ . Meanwhile, the fluorescence response ratio $(F/F_{0})$ enhanced with the increasing of $\mathtt{C u}$ -MOFs concentration, and no significant increase was observed over $150~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ (Fig. 3c). The loading efficiency was examined as $76.5\%$ when the concentration of $\mathbf{Cu-MOF}$ nanosheets was $150~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ (Fig. 3d). To further optimize the performance of the CuMOFs/aptamer nanoprobe, the temperature and $\mathsf{p H}$ of the reaction between Cu-MOFs/Aptamer and target ATP were explored. The results in Fig. 3e, f revealed that using $\mathrm{pH}6.5$ of PBS buffer at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ could reach the highest response in ATP detection.
Under optimum conditions, Cu-MOFs can be employed as a quantitative analysis nanoprobe for ATP detection. Figure 4a displays that the fluorescence signal is recovered as the increasing concentration of ATP, which is proportional to the ATP concentration ranging from $10~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ to $200~{\upmu\mathrm{M}}$ (Fig. 4b). The detection limit was tested as $4.24\upmu\mathrm{M}$ on the basis of $3\upsigma/$ slope ( $\overleftarrow{\upsigma}$ represents the standard deviation of the fluorescence signal for the blank measurements), indicating that Cu-MOFs/aptamer-based nanoprobe has great sensitivity for the monitor of intracellular ATP [27, 34, 35].
To study the selectivity of this Cu-MOFs/aptamer nanoprobe for the detection of ATP, other analogous including GTP, CTP, and UTP, were measured. As shown in Fig. 5, only a little fluorescence recovery is induced by these molecules, which is due to the high specific affinity of FAM-aptamer to ATP. The resulted complexes that formed from the specific binding between ATP and aptamer can release aptamer from Cu-MOFs nanosheets. At the same time, other analogues are invalid, so that the aptamer could not leave from Cu-MOFs nanosheets. These results proved the remarkable selectivity of CuMOFs/aptamer nanoprobe for ATP measurement in complicated environments.
## 3.3  Intracellular Imaging of ATP
To apply the Cu-MOFs/aptamer probe into the measuring of ATP level in living cells, the stability and biocompatibility of nanoprobe were examined [14, 36–38]. Initially, Cu-MOFs/aptamer nanoprobe was cultured with D-PBS buffer containing $10\%$ FBS or DNase I. In Fig. 6a, both the fluorescence intensity of Cu-MOFs/aptamer with $10\%$ FBS and DNase I showed no obvious enhancement even treated for $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ , suggesting that neither significant nonspecific desorption nor enzymatic degradation was caused, demonstrating that the stability of $\mathrm{Cu}$ -MOFs/aptamer to image ATP in complex biology environment. Furthermore, the cytotoxicity of the $\mathrm{Cu}$ -MOFs and $\mathrm{Cu}$ -MOFs/aptamer nanoprobe on HeLa cells was investigated by the MTT experiment [33]. In Fig. 6b, HeLa cells that treated with different concentration of Cu-MOFs of Cu-MOFs/aptamer nanoprobe remains cell viability above $85\%$ . These results implied that the CuMOFs/aptamer nanoprobe shows great potential to monitor and image ATP in living cells.
Then, Cu-MOFs/aptamer nanoprobe was cultured with HeLa cells, and the cell imaging for ATP was researched by CLSM. In Fig. 7b, the fluorescence response intensity of the cells cultured with Cu-MOFs/aptamer is clearly stronger than that of the untreated cells in Fig. 7a, testifying successful intracellular FAM-aptamer delivery, excellent specificity to ATP through the nanoprobe and resistance to non-target displacement. Moreover, to further demonstrate that the fluctuation of fluorescence intensity rested with the endogenic ATP, we modulated the intracellular ATP degree through drug stimulation experiments. As shown in Fig. 7c, the fluorescence intensity is reduced markedly when the cells were cultured with oligomycin producing a sudden depletion of ATP level [39, 40]. On the contrary, upon ATP increase by etoposide, a wellknown ATP inducer [41], a remarkable fluorescence accession is observed in Fig. 7d. All these results illustrated that the Cu-MOFs/Aptamer nanoprobe is a robust platform for in situ imaging ATP in living cells and monitoring the undulations of the intracellular ATP level.
## 4  Conclusion
In summary, an excellent 2D Cu-MOFs/aptamer nanoprobe was constructed for cellular ATP sensing and imaging. In the assay, 2D Cu-MOF nanosheets exhibit a large surface area, high active sites, strong absorption to ssDNA and excellent quenching abilities to FAM. Despite these properties of Cu-MOF nanosheets, the ability of avoiding non-target displacement make Cu-MOFs/aptamer as a useful platform for monitoring ATP in complicated biological media with high selectivity. The Cu-MOFs/aptamer was successfully applied in imaging the fluctuation of intracellular ATP trigged by simulate drugs. Overall, the 2D Cu-MOFs/aptamer-based assessment makes more promising in various applications in clinical diagnostics and biomedical studies.
